WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.960
Hearing is incredible.

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:06.660
Think about it: a tiny little bit of tissue
in your ear, a tenth of a millimetre thick,

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.770
moves in response to changes in air pressure,

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:11.250
and converts them into
tiny vibrations in fluid,

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.850
and then those get converted into electrochemical
impulses that go the brain,

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:21.180
where they are somehow decoded into
what you experience as sound.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.699
And if all of that is working properly,

00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:27.880
then you're able to pick out one source of
sound from another, in 3D space,

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.280
and your brain is optimised
to pick out words and language

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.760
even through huge amounts of noise.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:39.800
It'll even filter out constant, steady noise
like a fan or the wind whistling through trees.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.739
To give an idea of
just how difficult a task that is:

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.460
this is the waveform of the sound
that's on this video.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:49.539
Same information, just presented differently,

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:52.520
but it's not something that you can
make sense of with your eyes.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.619
And it's not like your eardrum only resonates
at a few frequencies.

00:00:55.619 --> 00:00:58.299
Human hearing in a
healthy, young person runs from

00:00:58.299 --> 00:01:03.260
somewhere up around 20,000 cycles a second,
all the way down to 20.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.840
Which means the eardrum can pick up
air movements with wavelengths

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.700
that range from a couple of centimetres for
high frequencies,

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:14.009
to 10 or 20 metres for really low bass.

00:01:14.009 --> 00:01:19.360
The sort of low, rumbling bass that you
would get from, say, a jet engine.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.049
Just over there is Amsterdam Schiphol airport.

00:01:22.049 --> 00:01:25.890
In terms of aircraft flying in and out,
it's the busiest airport in Europe.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.359
Six runways. Two control towers,

00:01:28.359 --> 00:01:33.020
one just for the new runway that's over there
that opened in 2003.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:36.320
That runway is five kilometres away from the
terminal.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:37.340
If you look at aerial photos,

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.799
you can see plane after plane after plane
lined up on a fifteen-minute taxi,

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.660
including going on a bridge
across a road and a canal.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:48.780
It was raining when my plane landed, but I
just about got that shot out the window.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.560
But there was one residential area,
just back there, where there was

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:56.009
nothing but flat ground between those houses
and the back end of jet engines.

00:01:56.009 --> 00:01:58.439
I mean, of course it was flat ground,
it's the Netherlands,

00:01:58.439 --> 00:02:01.260
but there was one spot just back there where it
got significantly louder

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.689
for the people who lived there.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:05.620
And those local residents were not happy.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.600
Of course, the one day I'm here,

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.720
they're actually landing on that runway,
not taking off

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.120
so the engines are pointed the other way,
but the point still stands.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.500
The local residents were not happy.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.640
This is the solution.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.400
Well, not a solution, 
at least something to help.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.680
This is a park that serves two purposes:
one, to be land art,

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:27.720
the sort of high-concept big sculpture
that's created

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.260
by moving enormous amounts of earth
and rock.

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:31.880
This was designed by an artist.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:35.340
I mean, I'd send up a drone to show you,
but, you know, airport.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.430
But the second purpose is sound dampening.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:42.230
These ridges are metres apart,
roughly, very roughly,

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:46.930
around the same wavelength as the low rumble
you get from a jet engine.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.290
Not exact, of course, but in the same order
of magnitude,

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.710
close enough to be disruptive.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.760
So rather than the sound waves just rolling
over the land,

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:59.290
instead they're scattered in all directions,
up and away, not onwards.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:04.150
Not perfectly, not even close, but enough
to bring the noise level at those houses

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:06.830
back down within acceptable limits.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:09.280
The architects claim that
one of the inspirations for this

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.090
was people noticing that the engine noise
sounded quieter when the fields were ploughed.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:17.780
Because, yes, your brain can filter out
constant, steady noise.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:22.060
It can even, over time, get used to filtering
out intermittent, loud noise,

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.480
like living next to an airport.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:30.640
But that filtering job is a lot easier if
the noise would just be a little less loud.

